-# PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT VERIFICATION - the missing half of "green means shipped".
-#
-# CI proves a commit is good; nothing proved it actually REACHED production. Twenty
-# consecutive Vercel production builds once failed while the repo stayed green, and nobody
-# was told (docs/DEPLOYMENT.md has the full story). This workflow closes that gap three ways:
-#
-#   1. Vercel reports a production deployment FAILED  -> a red run on that commit + the
-#      rolling issue below.
-#   2. Vercel reports a production deployment READY   -> fetch https://noacg.studio/
-#      version.json (written by scripts/write-version.mjs during the build) and verify the
-#      live commit IS this deployment's commit (or a newer main commit that overtook it),
-#      and that the landing page and editor answer. A deploy that "succeeded" but did not
-#      take effect is a red run, not a silent nothing.
-#   3. A scheduled drift check compares the live commit against main independently of any
-#      event - the belt against the failure mode where no deployment (and so no event) is
-#      created at all.
-#   4. A browser opens the deployed app on a printed `#/graphic/<id>` link and checks it is
-#      still there a moment later. Curl can only ask whether a page answers; this asks what the
-#      app DOES with the URL, which is where the failure of 2026-09-10 lived.
-#
-# Alerting is the weekly-audit pattern: ONE rolling issue ("Production is not running the
-# latest main commit"), one comment per newly failing commit - the same commit never alerts
-# twice - closed automatically by the next verified-healthy state.
-#
-# Which `deployment_status` counts as "Vercel reports" above is not obvious and is where this
-# workflow went wrong once - the `if:` on the verify job below carries that story.
